Problem:
The project examines how coaching participation, Class XII academic performance, and reported coaching duration relate to entrance-examination performance. The
supplied dataset contains 666 observations and 11 features plus the categorical target. It has no missing values. There are 44 exact duplicate feature rows; these were
retained because no student ID is provided, so identical attributes cannot prove that records represent the same candidate.

📌 Overview
Students :   666
Excellent:   15.2%
Good     :   31.5%
Vg       :   29.7%
Average  :   23.6%

Research Questions
# Question
1 Is participation in coaching associated with entrance-examination performance?
2 How is Class XII academic performance associated with entrance-examination performance?
3 Does reported coaching duration show different performance patterns?

Three Key Findings
1. Coaching: Coaching participation is associated with performance (χ²=29.09, df=6, p=5.85e-05; Cramer's V=0.148). The OA group has the highest observed Excellent
share (35.8%), while WA has the highest Vg share (32.5%). The effect is small and the analysis is observational, so this does not establish that coaching causes the
difference.
2. Class XII preparation: This is the strongest observed relationship (χ²=72.32, df=9, p=5.32e-12; Cramer's V=0.190). Because the Class XII Average category has only
12 students, a robustness check combining Average with Good still gives p=1.53e-13 and V=0.233, supporting the same conclusion.
3. Coaching duration: Duration is highly imbalanced: TWO=368, ONE=199, THREE=86, FOUR=11, FIVE=1, SEVEN=1. Sparse expected counts make the ordinary
chi-square approximation unreliable. A 20,000-repetition permutation test gives p≈0.218; therefore there is insufficient evidence of an association.

Recommended Action
Use prior academic performance to identify students who may benefit from targeted support. Treat coaching as one contextual factor rather than assuming it causes better
outcomes. To evaluate coaching effectiveness causally, collect longitudinal data or use a study design that better controls for prior achievement and access-related
confounding.

Limitations & Bias
The data are observational, so causal claims are not justified. Coaching choice may be confounded by prior academic achievement, family background, resources, or other unmeasured factors.
The supplied duration labels are categorical and their exact units are not documented, so they are treated only as reported categories. The dataset concerns candidates who qualified for a
medical entrance examination in Assam and may not generalize to all students.
